WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.450
A Zero Trust program usually builds
identity, devices and the network first.

2
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.762
Each of those controls passes a
legitimate user on a compliant device

3
00:00:09.762 --> 00:00:13.850
sending a file to an approved
destination, whatever the file holds.

4
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.225
Nothing upstream said
what the payload was.

5
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:21.350
CISA's Zero Trust Maturity Model makes the
same point with a Data pillar of its own.

6
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:24.250
Three things make perimeter
thinking fail for data.

7
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.511
It moves more than people do: an export
becomes an attachment, a OneDrive file,

8
00:00:29.511 --> 00:00:31.516
a chat paste and an AI summary,

9
00:00:31.516 --> 00:00:35.775
and each copy inherits none of
the original's access controls.

10
00:00:35.775 --> 00:00:39.225
Access is granted to
containers, not to content.

11
00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:42.100
And the agency can't protect
what it hasn't named.

12
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.825
Microsoft's sensitivity label
is what lets protection travel.

13
00:00:45.825 --> 00:00:49.860
It's stored in clear text in the
item's metadata, it stays with the

14
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.050
content wherever it's saved, and
so does any encryption it applies.

15
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:57.550
But a label is only as good
as the class it stands for.
